# New‐Generation Anion‐Pillared Metal–Organic Frameworks with Customized Cages for Highly Efficient CO2 Capture

## abstract
The rational design of porous materials for CO2 capture under realistic process conditions is highly desirable. However, trade‐offs exist among a nanopore's capacity, selectivity, adsorption heat, and stability. In this study, a new generation of anion‐pillared metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs) are reported with customizable cages for benchmark CO2 capture from flue gas. The optimally designed TIFSIX‐Cu‐TPA exhibits a high CO2 capacity, excellent CO2/N2 selectivity, high thermal stability, and chemical stability in acid solution and acidic atmosphere, as well as modest adsorption heat for facile regeneration. Additionally, the practical separation performance of the synthesized MOFs is demonstrated by breakthrough experiments under various process conditions. A highly selective separation is achieved at 298–348 K with the impressive CO2 capacity of 2.1–1.4 mmol g−1. Importantly, the outstanding performance is sustained under high humidity and over ten repeat process cycles. The molecular mechanism of MOF's CO2 adsorption is further investigated in situ by CO2 dosed single crystal structure and theoretical calculations, highlighting two separate binding sites for CO2 in small and large cages featured with high CO2 selectivity and loading, respectively. The simultaneous adsorption of CO2 inside these two types of interconnected cages accounts for the high performance of these newly designed anionic pillar‐caged MOFs.

New-Generation Anion-Pillared Metal–Organic Frameworks with Customized Cages for Highly Efficient $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Capture
Dedicated to Professor Rajamani Krishna
## 1. Introduction
Fossil fuel-based global energy production has led to massive emissions of carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ causing environmental issues such as climate change.[1,2] In addition to research and development of renewable energy sources, new materials and methods for the cost-efficient capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are needed for achieving net zero emission goals. Stateof-the-art, industrial-scale systems rely on physical and chemical absorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by amines. Although $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ removal is effective, this method is limited by an energy penalty $(\approx105\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ due to the amine’s thermal regeneration and by amine’s chemical decomposition.[3,4]
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a new class of porous solid-state materials, have emerged as promising adsorbents for gas separation due to their tailorable pore properties.[5–12] To date, a large number of MOFs have been investigated for their potential in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.[13–32] Incorporating unsaturated open metal sites[19,20] and grafting polar amino groups[21–26] are two common strategies to enhance $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption uptake or separation selectivity. However, poor stability, moderate selectivity, and high adsorption heat render most of MOFs unsuitable for industrial applications.
Anion pillared metal–organic frameworks (APMOFs) are a special class of
MOFs that consist of anionic pillars $(\mathrm{MF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ , $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Si}$ Ti or Ge; ${\mathrm{NbOF}}_{5}{^{2-}}$ , etc.), cationic metal nodes $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+},\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , etc.) and diverse organic ligands (pyrazine, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, dipyridin-4-yldiazene, etc.).[33–50] Due to the introduction of inorganic anions, the APMOFs possess polar pore surface and can interact with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by strong $\mathrm{F}\cdots\mathrm{C}$ electrostatic attraction, thus yielding an excellent selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}_{2}.^{[35-50]}$ As reported by the Eddaoudi group, there have been three generations of APMOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture (Scheme 1).[36] SIFSIX-3-Zn,[37] SIFSIX-3-Cu,[38] and $\mathrm{SIFSIX}{-}3{-}\mathrm{Ni}^{[39]}$ are the first generation. These APMOFs show remarkable high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity at $15\ \mathrm{kPa}$ (the partial pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the flue gas) and extremely high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity compared with other MOFs based on pure organic ligands. Compared to the regeneration energy of ${\approx}105\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for amine scrubber, the adsorption enthalpy of ${\approx}45{-}54\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ in these SIFSIX frameworks is favorable. However, these APMOFs suffer from poor chemical stability caused by their sensitivity to moisture, limiting their practical application for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Specifically, the presence of $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in flue gas requires adsorbents with high chemical tolerance in humid and corrosive environments. The second generation of APMOFs, NbOFFIVE-1-Ni,[40] and AlFFIVE-1-Ni[41] addressed these issues by introducing anion pillars with higher nucleophilicity, $[\mathrm{NbOF}_{5}]^{2-}$ or $[\mathrm{AlF}_{5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]^{2-}$ . These two APMOFs enhanced thermo- and chemo-stability and featured the benchmark performance for direct $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from air due to their extremely high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivities. However, the latter typically results in extremely high adsorption heats, increasing the cost of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ regeneration. Additionally, due to the modest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of NbOFFIVE-1-Ni and AlFFIVE-1-Ni at $15\mathrm{kPa}$ , these two MOFs are not ideal for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation that requires a high capture rate (e.g., from flue gas). In 2019, Eddaoudi and team reported the third generation of APMOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas.[36] The interpenetrated dptz- $\mathrm{\cdotCuTiF_{6}}$ exhibited a high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption amount at $15\ \mathrm{kPa}$ , low adsorption heat $(38.3\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ , as well as good resistance over humidity. However, $\mathrm{dptz.CuTiF}_{6}$ decomposes above $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and is unstable in water environment.[42] Therefore, these APMOFs along with other similar candidates for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture[43–50] display certain drawbacks that limit their practical application.
To evaluate quantitatively the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture potential in flue gas in particular, we propose six metrics: $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity at $15\mathrm{\kPa}$ , adsorption capacity at $100~\mathrm{kPa}$ , IAST selectivity for $15/85\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , thermal stability, chemical stability, and adsorption heat $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ . To be relevant from an application perspective, an ideal adsorbent should exhibit a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of ${>}50$ STP $\mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at $15~\mathrm{{kPa}}$ and $>100$ STP $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{~kPa}$ , IAST selectivity of ${>}500$ , thermal stability ${>}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , chemical stability in acid solution and acid vapor, as well as low adsorption heat $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ of ${<}40\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . To the best of our knowledge, so far there is no solid adsorbent that fulfills all these requirements.
Given existing knowledge of APMOFs, we are motivated to adopt the bottom-up approach for designing a new generation of APMOFs, in order to realize the unmet requirements.
All APMOFs reported previously for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture are based on pillar-layered structures displaying uniform 1D channels/ pores. Generally, the confined channels $(<4\mathring\mathrm{A})$ afford stronger $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -framework interactions, i.e., high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption heat and high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity but lower saturated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity, while the expanded channels $(>4\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}})$ provide more space for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ accommodation, i.e., high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity but lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption heat and lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity. Based on these facts, we propose a new type of APMOF with both small and large pores that provide high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity, high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity, and modest adsorption heat for regeneration. Moreover, we suggest an organic linker with a large number of coordination bonds with metal nodes, which could enhance the framework stability. In the following, we report a new generation of APMOFs based on pillar structures with customized cages for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Four iso-structural MOFs, TIFSIX-Cu-TPA, SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, GeFSIX-Cu-TPA, and NbOFFIVE-Cu-TPA (A-Cu-TPA) were prepared and tested for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation. TIFSIX-Cu-TPA, SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, and GeFSIX-Cu-TPA fulfill simultaneously the requirements of high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity (at both 15 and $100\ \mathrm{kPa})$ , high IAST selectivity, and low adsorption heat, among which TIFSIX-Cu-TPA further fulfills the demands of thermal stability ${>}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and long chemical stability in acid solution and acid vapor.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Single crystals of A-Cu-TPA ( $\mathrm{A}=$ TIFSIX, SIFSIX, GeFSIX, and NbOFFIVE) were all produced by layering a MeOH solution of tri(pyridin-4yl)amine (TPA) onto the corresponding aqueous solution of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ salts (Figure  1a). The four MOFs are isomorphous and crystallize in highly symmetrical cubic $\mathrm{Pm}{\cdot}3\mathrm{n}$ space group. These frameworks consist of large icosahedral cages $(\approx8.5\mathrm{\AA})$ and small tetrahedral cages $(\approx4\ \bar{\mathrm{\AA}})$ that are not present in previous generations of APMOFs. Each large icosahedral cage contains twelve windows connected to the twelve neighboring small cages while every small tetrahedral cage has four windows shared with four neighboring large cages. Both large cages (with six anions) and small cages (with two anions) are abundant of Lewis basic F functional sites on their surface for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Such a structure with interlaced small and large cages are distinct from those of pillar-layered MOFs with straight 1D channels (Scheme 1). It is well known that the sizes of small/large cages as well as interconnecting windows can play a pivot role for gas adsorption, permeation, and selectivity.
Because of the tiny size difference $(<0.1\mathring\mathrm{A})$ of various anions, the cage diameter for these isostructural A-Cu-TPA MOFs is almost identical $_{<0.1}$ Å difference). However, these anions have different coordination ability and different interaction affinity toward guest molecules. Consequently, the thermal stability, chemical stability, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture performance may vary among them.
First, we investigated the thermal and chemical stability of A-Cu-TPA by thermal gravimetric analyisis (TGA) and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) since flue gas is under high temperature $(>50^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and contains trace acid gas. As shown in Figure 1b, all frameworks have a good thermal stability of over $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Particularly, TIFSIX-Cu-TPA exhibits the highest thermal stability of ${\approx}308~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The PXRD patterns and photographs of single crystals further showed that all these materials are highly stable in humid air and water (Figure 1c; Figures S3–S5, Supporting Information). TIFSIX- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{cu}$ -TPA was even stable after soaking in diluted aqueous HCl solution or exposing to humid HCl vapor at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Notably, all four materials retained the crystallinity after heating at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and under vacuum for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Such high stability endows them great potential for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas.
To confirm the excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture potential in TIFSIXCu-TPA, degassed crystalline TIFSIX-Cu-TPA was exposed to pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas at ${\approx}120\ \mathrm{kPa}$ for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ and characterized by single crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure 1d). We found that, on average, 18 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules are adsorbed in each unit cell of TIFSIX-CuTPA, corresponding to $3.0\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ per $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ anion. Different from all previous generations of APMOFs, two different $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding sites were observed – sites I and II. Site I is in the small cage and site II is in the large cage. All $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules in these two sites are trapped by $\scriptstyle\mathrm{F}\cdots\cdot\mathrm{C}=0$ electrostatic interactions. In Site I, every $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is trapped by four $\scriptstyle\mathrm{F}\cdots\cdot\mathrm{C}=0$ electrostatic interaction from two opposite $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ anions in the distances of $2.89-$ $3.83\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . In Site II, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ only interacts with one $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ anion and the F···C distance is 3.12–3.82  Å due to the disorder of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules. It should be noted that the small cage is not large enough to accommodate two $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules together. Although two symmetric positions for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules can be refined in the small cage, the occupancy is only $50\%$ . The number ratio of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules distributed inside small cages and inside large cages is 1:2, indicating that large cages provide more accessible space for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ accommodation.
Inspired by the unique $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -dosed single crystal structure, we further explored the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, by collecting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of four MOFs at eight temperatures in the range from 278 to 348  K. At 298  K and $15~\mathrm{kPa}$ , the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes were 51.7, 56.0, 54.8 and $37.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for TIFSIX-Cu-TPA, SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, GeFSIX-Cu-TPA, and NbOFFIVE-Cu-TPA, respectively (Figure 2a,b; Figures S10–S12, Supporting Information). Among them, the uptakes of TIFSIXCu-TPA, SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, and GeFSIX-Cu-TPA are comparable with those of many top-performing MOFs in the context of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, such as SIFSIX-3-Zn $(54.4\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,[37] NbOFFIVE-1-Ni $(50.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,[40] and dptz- ${\mathrm{CuSiF}}_{6}$ $(56.0\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,[36] but lower than dptz- ${\mathrm{CuTiF}}_{6}$ $(74.6~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .[36] At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $100~\mathrm{{kPa}}$ , the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes increase to 105.4, 107.9, 106.7, and $72.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, while the uptakes of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ are all $<9.0\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . These $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of MFSIX-Cu-TPA $(\mathbf{M}\:=\:\mathrm{Ti}$ , Si, Ge) are much higher than those of SIFSIX-3-Zn $(57.8\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,[37] NbOFFIVE1-Ni $(50.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,[40] and $\mathrm{dptz–CuSiF}_{6}$ $(90.5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,[36] and slightly surpass dptz- ${\mathrm{CuTiF}}_{6}$ $(101.2~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .[36] Notably, with the temperature rising from 278 to 348  K, the uptakes of TIFSIX-Cu-TPA at $15~\mathrm{kPa}$ decrease from 65.1 to $34.3~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , showing that these MOFs can adapt to the flue gas condition with different temperatures (Figure  2b). As selectivity is also an important parameter to assess the separation performance, we further calculated the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities on A-Cu-TPA using the ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) after fitting isotherms into dual-site Langmuir (for $\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ ) or single-site Langmuir (for ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}^{\prime}$ ) equation with excellent accuracy. The IAST selectivities for $15/85\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ are 910, 976, and 924 for TIFSIXCu-TPA, SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, and GeFSIX-Cu-TPA, comparable with other benchmark materials (Figures S13–S16, Supporting Information).
The isosteric heat of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ is an intrinsic parameter for evaluating the adsorption affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as well as the energy penalty for regeneration. Therefore, a modestly high $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ $\mathrm{~;~}(35{-}40\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-}})$ is important for the MOFs used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Figure  2c shows that the $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ values of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ calculated from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at eight temperatures for TIFSIX-Cu-TPA, SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, GeFSIX-Cu-TPA, and NbOFFIVE-Cu-TPA are $39.2\substack{-37.8}\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ under near-zero loading. These $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ values are much lower than aqueous amine $(105~\mathrm{~kJ~}~\mathrm{~mol^{-1}})$ ,[36] een-MOF/Al-Si $(73~\mathrm{~kJ~}~\mathrm{~mol^{-1}})$ ,[22] mmen$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ $(71\mathrm{~kJ~}\mathrm{~mol^{-1}}$ ),[23] CD-MOF-2 $(67.2~\mathrm{~kJ~}\mathrm{~mol}^{-1})$ ,[24] MOF-808-Gly $(46~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}})^{[28]}$ with ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}$ on the surface and Mg-MOF-74 $(47\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})^{[2}$ 2] with OMS, as well as ALF $(47.9\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mathrm{kJ}\ m o l^{-1}}})$ ).[27] Besides, these $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ values are also lower than those of many APMOFs such as NbOFFIVE-1-Ni $(54.6\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}),$ [40] SIFSIX-3-Cu $(54\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ,[38] SIFSIX-18-Ni- $\beta$ $(52\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ ,[43] CROFOUR-1-Ni $(50\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ ,[44] AlFFIVE-1-Ni $(47\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ ,[41] and SIFSIX-3-Zn $(45\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}}$ ),[37] and comparable with that of commercialized CALF-20 $(39\mathrm{~kJ~}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})^{[2}$ 8] for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas (Figure 2d). Notably, the overall shape of $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ curves signatured with two plateaus suggests that all the four MOFs in this study possess two distinct binding sites over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loading, consistent with observations from the single crystal structure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -dosed TIFSIX-Cu-TPA.
To further gain insight into the molecular mechanism of the unary $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ gas adsorption in these APMOFs, we carried out Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption in SIFSIX-Cu-TPA. Remarkably, without any fitting parameters, our results from GCMC simulations based on DDEC partial charges for Coulomb interactions and UFF forcefield for van der Waals interaction (see Experimental Section for details) overall agree with experimental ones (Figure  3a). At two temperatures (278 and 298  K), the simulated loadings for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are consistent with experimental results at both intermediate and high pressures (Figure  3a). However, at low pressures (e.g., $0.1\ \mathrm{kPa}$ ), simulation results are slightly larger than experimental ones, which indicates that our simulation protocols $(\mathrm{DDEC+UFF})$ overestimate the binding energies of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at site-I and site-II of SIFSIX-Cu-TPA. Consequently, the inset of Figure  3a shows that the calculated adsorption heats from GCMC are moderately larger than experimental data (Figure 2c), although overall shapes of curves are similar. It is worth mentioning that the zero-kelvin density-functional-theory (DFT) calculations provide the upper bounds for adsorption heat, which are 53.8 and $38.5\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ bound at site-I and site-II, respectively. For $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption, our GCMC results overall are consistent with experimental ones (Figure  3a), except for slight overpredictions at high pressures.
From simulation trajectory of GCMC, we found possible binding conformations of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at site-I and site-II. The small cage harbors two site-Is, as shown in Figure 3b. However, only one $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can occupy the small cage at any time, limited by the cage size. Therefore, consistent with the crystal structure, two possible poses of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ inside the small cage were identified from the simulation trajectories at two different pressures (Figure 3b; Movies S1 and S2 for 0.1 and $100\mathrm{~kPa}$ , respectively, Supporting Information). Figure  3c shows a similar binding pose of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at the site-II inside a large cage. We carried out DFT calculations to optimize the binding structure at $0\mathrm{~K~}$ and results (similar to experimental ones) are shown in Figure S19 (Supporting Information) for comparisons. Generally, each bound $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule orients its conformation at either site-I or site-II so that the C atom in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is close to F atoms in the binding site due to the favorable electrostatic attraction. Meanwhile, the electrostatic repulsion drives the O atoms in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ further away from F atoms in the binding site (Figure 3b,c).
Given the highly inhomogeneous adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in SIFSIX- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{Cu}$ -TPA that is ignored by IAST, we further performed GCMC simulations for the dual gas adsorption for a mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (with the mole ratio of 15:85 as in flue gas). As shown in Figure S18 (Supporting Information), we found that the calculated selectivities from GCMC results are consistent with and smaller than predictions of IAST at low and high pressures, respectively.
To evaluate the potential for practical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas, dynamic breakthrough experiments under various conditions were conducted. For each of four MOFs, a convincing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation was achieved. The breakthrough experiments using 15/85 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ mixture were carried out at 298, 323, and $348\mathrm{~K~}$ for TIFSIX-Cu-TPA to imitate the flue gas conditions (Figure  4a). At all temperatures, $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ was eluted almost immediately while $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was retained for 33.6, 27.2, and $20.8\ \mathrm{\min}$ at 298, 323, and 348  K, respectively. Correspondingly, the calculated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes are 2.1, 1.7, and $\mathrm{1.4\mmol{\g}^{-1}}$ . Notably, the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity $(35.4\%)$ with the temperature rising (from 298 to 348 K) is much lower than those of the third-generation materials (e.g., dptz- $\mathrm{CuTiF_{6}}$ : $51.5\%$ , from 298 to 328  K),[36] further indicating that TIFSIX-Cu-TPA can deal with flue gas under a wide range of temperatures. Considering the remarkable stability of TIFSIX-Cu-TPA, we further conducted the breakthrough experiments under humid conditions (Figure 4b). We found that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separation performance was maintained completely under a high relative humid of $80\%$ . As recyclability is very important for the practical use, our breakthrough experiments were repeated for 10 cycles and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity displayed a negligible loss (Figure  4c). Compared with the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $2.1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in TIFSIX-Cu-TPA, the captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ amount in SIFSIX-Cu-TPA (2.3  mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ and GeFSIX-Cu-TPA $(2.2\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ are slightly higher while that of NbOFFIVE- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{cu}$ -TPA $(1.8\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is lower, which is consistent with the sequence of the uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in static adsorption experiment (Figure  4d). Additionally, SIFSIX-CuTPA, GeFSIX-Cu-TPA, and NbOFFIVE- $\mathrm{\DeltaCu}$ -TPA also display a good recyclability and complete retaining of the separation performance under humid conditions (Figures  S23–S25, Supporting Information). In brief, the combination of high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity at different temperature, preserved separation performance under humid conditions and excellent recycling capacity render A-Cu-TPA as a new generation of promising adsorbents for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, we reported a new generation of anion pillared MOFs with customized cages for benchmark $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas. The combination of large $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity under both low and high pressures (51.7 and 105.4 STP $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 15 and $100\ \mathrm{kPa}\$ ), excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ IAST selectivity (910, 15/85), ultrahigh thermal stability $(308~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , and chemical stability in acid solution and atmosphere, as well as moderate adsorption enthalpy $(39.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ for regeneration has been realized for the first time by TIFSIX-Cu-TPA in particular. Meanwhile, the efficient practical separation of mixed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gases has also been confirmed by column-breakthrough experiments under different conditions. The captured amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reaches 2.1–1.4  mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ from 298 to $348\ \mathrm{K}.$ The performance is sustained over 10 cycles and under humid conditions. Distinguishably from all other APMOFs, we revealed that for newly designed APMOFs in this study there exist two binding sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by the analysis of in situ gas-loaded single crystal structure, GCMC simulations, and DFT calculations. Physically, the observed physisorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on these anionic sites is energetically driven by the favorable charge–quadrupole interaction between an anion and a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule. In general, TIFSIX-CuTPA, as well as other isostructures SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, GeFSIXCu-TPA, and NbOFFIVE-Cu-TPA, are promising and highly efficient alternative MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas under complex realistic conditions.
## 4. Experimental Section
Preparation of Crystals of TIFSIX-Cu-TPA: TIFSIX-Cu-TPA was prepared according to the reported literature.[9] To a $5m L$ long thin tube was added with $\mathsf{C u T i F}_{6}{\bullet}{\mathsf{x H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $(\approx].6{\ m g})$ ) in aqueous solution $(7m L)$ . ${\mathsf{M e O H}}/$ $H_{2}O$ mixture $(\mathsf{v};\mathsf{v}=\mathsf{l};\mathsf{l})$ , $2m L$ ) was slowly layered above the solution, followed by MeOH solution $(7m L)$ of TPA $\scriptstyle(\approx1.0\ m g)$ . The tube was sealed and left undisturbed at $298\mathsf{K}$ . After ${\approx}1$ week, purple single crystals were obtained.
Preparation of Crystals of SIFSIX-Cu-TPA: SIFSIX- $\mathsf{C u}$ -TPA was prepared according to the reported literature.[51] To a $5m L$ long thin tube was added with $\mathsf{C u S i F}_{6}{\bullet}{\mathsf{x}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $(\approx1.5~\mathsf{m g})$ in aqueous solution (1 mL). ${\mathsf{M e O H}}/$ $H_{2}O$ mixture $(\mathsf{v};\mathsf{v}=\mathsf{l};\mathsf{l}$ , $2m L$ ) was slowly layered above the solution, followed by MeOH solution $(7m L)$ of TPA $\scriptstyle(\approx1.0\ m g)$ . The tube was sealed and left undisturbed at $298\ \mathsf{K}.$ . After ${\approx}7$ week, dark violet single crystals were obtained.
Preparation of Crystals of GeFSIX- $C u$ -TPA: To a $5~\mathsf{m L}$ long thin tube was added with ${\mathsf{C u C e F}}_{6}{\cdot}{\mathsf{x H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $_{(\approx1.7~\mathsf{m g})}$ in aqueous solution $(7m L)$ . ${\mathsf{M e O H}}/{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ mixture ( $\mathrm{v}{:}\mathsf{v}=\mathsf{l}{:}\mathsf{l}$ , 2  mL) was slowly layered above the solution, followed by MeOH solution $(7~\mathsf{m L})$ of TPA $_{(\approx1.0\ m g)}$ . The tube was sealed and left undisturbed at $298\ \mathsf{K}.$ After $\approx$ one week, purple single crystals were obtained.
Preparation of Crystals of NbOFFIVE- $C u$ -TPA: To a 5 mL long thin tube was added with $\mathsf{C u N b O F}_{5}{\cdot}{\times}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(\approx1.8{\ m g})$ in aqueous solution $(7~\mathsf{m L})$ . ${\mathsf{M e O H}}/{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ mixture ( $\varsigma_{\sf N}:\sf{v}=\sf{l}:\sf{l}$ , $2m L$ ) was slowly layered above the solution, followed by MeOH solution $(7m L)$ of TPA $(\approx].0\ m g)$ . The tube was sealed and left undisturbed at $298\ \mathsf{K}.$ . After ${\approx}7$ week, purple single crystals were obtained.
Preparation of Crystals of $C O_{2}$ Loaded TIFSIX-Cu-TPA: Crystals of $\mathsf{T}|\mathsf{F}\mathsf{S}|\mathsf{X}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{u}$ -TPA was filled into a glass tube and heated at ${120~^{\circ}C}$ under vacuum for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling down to $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was introduced into with Builder SSA 7000 (Beijing) instrument until the pressure reach to $\mathsf{l}20\ \mathsf{k P a}$ and the state was maintained for $\textsf{l h}$ . Then the crystals were picked out and covered with the degassed oil for single-crystal X-ray diffraction measurements.
Single-Crystal $\boldsymbol{x}$ -Ray Diffraction: Single-crystal X-ray diffraction studies were conducted on the BrukerAXS D8 VENTURE diffractometer equipped with a PHOTON-II detector (MoKα, $\lambda=0.77073\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Indexing was performed using APEX2. Data integration and reduction were completed using SaintPlus 6.01. Absorption correction was performed by the multi-scan method implemented in SADABS. The space group was determined using XPREP implemented in APEX2. The structure was solved with SHELXS-97 (direct methods) and refined on F2 (nonlinear least-squares method) with SHELXL-97 contained in APEX2, WinGX v1.70.01, and OLEX2 v1.1.5 program packages. All non-hydrogen atoms were refined anisotropically. The contribution of disordered solvent molecules was treated as diffuse using the Squeeze routine implemented in Platon.
Powder X-Ray Diffraction: PXRD data were collected on the SHIMADZU XRD-6000 diffractometer $(\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha\lambda=7.540598\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ ) with an operating power of 40  KV, $30~\mathsf{m A}$ and a scan speed of $4.0^{\circ}\ \mathsf{m i n^{-1}}$ . The range of $2\theta$ was from $5^{\circ}$ to $50^{\circ}$ .
Thermal Gravimetric Analysis: Thermal gravimetric analysis was performed on the TGA STA449F5 instrument. Experiments were carried out using a platinum pan under nitrogen atmosphere which conducted by a flow rate of $60~\mathsf{m L}~\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ nitrogen gas. The data were collected at the temperature range of $50{\mathrm{-}}600^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ with a ramp of $10^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ .
The Static Gas Adsorption Equilibrium Measurements: The static ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption equilibrium were measured on the Builder SSA 7000 (Beijing) instrument. Before gas adsorption measurements, the sample $\scriptstyle(\approx\log\mid00\ m g)$ was evacuated at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2\ h$ first, and then at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $12\ h$ until the pressure dropped below $7\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathsf{H}\upxi$ . The sorption isotherms were collected at 77, 278, 288, 298, 308, 318, 328, 338, and $348\mathrm{~K~}$ on activated samples. The experimental temperatures were controlled by liquid nitrogen bath (77 K) or water bath (278–348 K), respectively. The water vapor adsorption equilibrium was measured on the BeiShiDe DVS (Beijing) instrument.
Breakthrough Experiments: The breakthrough experiments were carried out on a dynamic gas breakthrough equipment HPMC-41 (Xuzhou). The experiments were conducted using a stainless-steel column ( $4.6\ m m$ inner diameter $\times50m m$ length). The weight of TIFSIX-Cu-TPA, SIFSIX-Cu-TPA, GeFSIX- $\mathsf{C u}$ -TPA, and NbOFFIVE-Cu-TPA packed in the columns was 0.55, 0.51, 0.58, and $0.56\ \mathrm{g}$ , respectively. The column was activated at $75^{\circ}C$ for $2h$ under vacuum, and then raised to ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for overnight. The mixed gas of ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ (15/85, v/v) was then introduced. Outlet gas from the column was monitored using gas chromatography (GC-9860-5CNJ) with the thermal conductivity detector TCD. After the breakthrough experiment, the sample was regenerated with an Ar flow of $5~\mathsf{m L}\mathsf{m i n^{-1}}$ under $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $5\textmd{h}$ .
Fitting of Experimental Data on Pure Component Isotherms: All the unary isotherms for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ measured at eight different temperatures (278– 348  K) in A-Cu-TPA were fitted with excellent accuracy using the dualsite Langmuir model, where two distinct adsorption sites A and B are distinguished:
$$
q=\frac{q_{\mathsf{s a t},A}b_{\mathsf{A P}}}{1+b_{\mathsf{A P}}}+\frac{q_{\mathsf{s a t},\mathsf{B}}b_{B}\mathsf{p}}{1+b_{\mathsf{B P}}}
$$
In Equation  (1), the Langmuir parameters $b_{A},b_{B}$ are temperature dependent.
$$
b_{\mathsf{A}}=b_{\mathsf{A0}}\exp\left({\frac{E_{\mathsf{A}}}{R T}}\right)\quad b_{\mathsf{b}}=b_{\mathsf{B0}}\exp\left({\frac{E_{\mathsf{B}}}{R T}}\right)
$$
In Equation (2), $E_{A},E_{B}$ are the energy parameters associated with sites A, and B, respectively.
All the unary isotherms for $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ measured at eight different temperatures (278–348 K) in $\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{C u}$ -TPA were fitted with excellent accuracy using the single-site Langmuir model.
$$
q=\frac{q_{s a t}b p}{1+b p}
$$
The unary isotherm fit parameters for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ are provided in Equation (3).
IAST Calculations: The adsorption selectivity for separation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ is defined by
$$
S_{\mathrm{ads}}=\frac{q_{1}/q_{2}}{p_{1}/p_{2}}
$$
where $\mathsf{q}_{1},\mathsf{q}_{2}$ are the molar loading (units: mol $\displaystyle{\mathsf{k g}^{-1}})$ ) of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ in the adsorbed phase in equilibrium with a gas mixture with partial pressures p1, ${\mathsf p}_{2}$ in the bulk gas.
Calculation of Isosteric Heat of Adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ : The isosteric heat of adsorption, $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}},$ is defined as
$$
Q_{\mathsf{s t}}=-R T^{2}{\Bigg(}{\frac{\partial{\mathsf{l n}}p}{\partial T}}{\Bigg)}_{q}
$$
where the derivative in the right member of Equation (5) is determined at constant adsorbate loading, $\boldsymbol{q}$ . The calculations are based on the Clausius–Clapeyron equation.
Calculation of Dynamic Adsorption Capacity: The amount of gas adsorbed i $(q_{\mathrm{i}})$ is calculated from the breakthrough curves using the following:
$$
q_{\mathrm{i}}=\frac{C_{\mathrm{i}}V}{22.4\times m}\times\int\displaylimits_{0}^{t}{\left(1-\frac{F}{F_{0}}\right)}d t
$$
here, $q_{\mathrm{i}}$ is the equilibrium adsorption capacity of gas $i$ $(\mathsf{m m o l\textsubscript{g}}^{-1})$ , $C_{\mathrm{i}}$ is the feed gas concentration, V is the volumetric feed flow rate $(m L\min^{-1})$ , t is the adsorption time (min), $F_{0}$ and $F$ are the inlet and outlet gas molar flow rates, respectively, and $m$ is the mass of the adsorbent (g).
Density Functional Theory Calculation: Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were carried out to optimize atomic structures of synthesized MOF (SIFSIX-Cu-TPA) as well as the one with a bound ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule, using CP2K package.[52] The Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) exchange functional,[53] Gaussian plane wave (PAW) pseudopotentials,[54] and DZVP basis sets[55] for carbon, oxygen, fluorine, nitrogen, silicon, and copper atoms, were used to describe the exchange–correlation interactions and electron–ion interaction, respectively. The PBE-D3 method[56] with Becke–Jonson damping for all atoms was used for the van der Waals correction. The Hubbard $U$ correction for the open-shell 3d transition metal $(\mathsf{C u})$ was used for geometry optimizations. The $\upsilon$ value of $5.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ was used in this study. In all calculations, the entire simulation systems were neutral. The adsorption energy for one ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule on site-1 or site-2 can be obtained by comparing total energies for the $M O F.C O_{2}$ complex, MOF-only, and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -only.
Grand Canonical Monte Carlo Calculations: The software package ${\mathsf{R A S P A}}^{[57]}$ was applied to perform Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations to obtain adsorption isotherms. The MOF (SIFSIX-Cu-TPA) in simulation was treated as a rigid sorbent and its interaction energies with adsorbed gas molecules include both van der Waals and electrostatic contributions. The UFF force field[58] was chosen for calculating van der Waals energies. For computing electrostatic energies, DDEC6 atomic charges[59] were obtained from DFT calculations (see above). The Trappe force field[60] was used for gas molecules $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{C O}_{2}}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{\Omega}$ ). It was found that this combination of force fields yielded the isotherms consistent with experimental ones. Simulations were performed for both unary $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ or $\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}_{2})$ and binary $(r_{\mathsf C\mathsf{O}2}:r_{\mathsf{N}2}=75{:}85)$ gas adsorptions, where $r$ was the mole concentration in bulk. From the simulation of binary gas adsorption, the concentrations $r^{\prime}$ of adsorbed gases inside the MOF were obtained. The selectivity was calculated as $(r_{\mathsf{C O2}}^{\prime}/r_{\mathsf{N2}}^{\prime})/(r_{\mathsf{C O2}}/r_{\mathsf{N2}})$ .
